{"qid":"Q962","names":["Republic of Benin"],"title":"Republic of Benin","endonym":null,"capsule":"The modern Republic of Benin takes its name from a 1990 constitutional change that ended the one-party People's Republic of Benin established in 1975 under Mathieu Kérékou's Marxist-Leninist military government. A national conference in February 1990, prompted by economic collapse and popular unrest, produced a new democratic constitution; in March 1991 Kérékou lost to Nicéphore Soglo in what became the first peaceful electoral transfer of power by a sitting head of state in continental African history. The country's politics have since alternated competitive multi-party elections with Kérékou returning to the presidency in 1996 and 2001, followed by Boni Yayi (2006–2016) and Patrice Talon (2016–present). Benin remains notable for the restoration of its democracy despite persistent developmental challenges.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Benin's internationally recognised borders are well established through colonial-era Franco-British delimitations and are not currently disputed.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Benin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Mathieu Kérékou","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathieu_K%C3%A9r%C3%A9kou"},{"label":"Wikipedia: 1991 Beninese presidential election","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1991_Beninese_presidential_election"}],"britannica":"place/Benin","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Benin","rulers":[{"qid":"Q438852","name":"Justin Ahomadégbé-Tomêtin","start":1964,"end":1965,"image":"Ahomadegbe.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commo